<article class="env-lemma" id="03F3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03F3">Lemma <span data-tag="03F3">21.7.1</span>.</a> Let $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ be a ringed site. Let $U$ be an object of $\mathcal{C}$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $\mathcal{I}$ is an injective $\mathcal{O}$-module then $\mathcal{I}|_ U$ is an injective $\mathcal{O}_ U$-module. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For any sheaf of $\mathcal{O}$-modules $\mathcal{F}$ we have $H^ p(U, \mathcal{F}) = H^ p(\mathcal{C}/U, \mathcal{F}|_ U)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Recall that the functor $j_ U^{-1}$ of restriction to $U$ is a right adjoint to the functor $j_{U!}$ of extension by $0$, see Modules on Sites, Section <a href="/tag/03DH" data-tag="03DH">18.19</a>. Moreover, $j_{U!}$ is exact. Hence (1) follows from Homology, Lemma <a href="/tag/015Z" data-tag="015Z">12.29.1</a>. 
    <p>
      By definition $H^ p(U, \mathcal{F}) = H^ p(\mathcal{I}^\bullet (U))$ where $\mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{I}^\bullet $ is an injective resolution in $\textit{Mod}(\mathcal{O})$. By the above we see that $\mathcal{F}|_ U \to \mathcal{I}^\bullet |_ U$ is an injective resolution in $\textit{Mod}(\mathcal{O}_ U)$. Hence $H^ p(U, \mathcal{F}|_ U)$ is equal to $H^ p(\mathcal{I}^\bullet |_ U(U))$. Of course $\mathcal{F}(U) = \mathcal{F}|_ U(U)$ for any sheaf $\mathcal{F}$ on $\mathcal{C}$. Hence the equality in (2). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>